@@ -708,7 +708,7 @@ Selected inputs retain their exact bytes and SHA-256 digests: the Brief, existin
 1. Validates an optional `--name`, then the Brief. An explicit Task Name is safe lowercase kebab-case, at most 24 characters, and cannot be a Task ID; it need not match the title. When omitted, Posse derives a short display name from the title or uses `task` when no safe ASCII name can be formed. Repeated names are allowed, but lookup by a repeated name or title returns `task_ambiguous`; `t<n>` is the stable selector. For Change Tasks, the title uses conventional-commit form `type[(scope)][!]: description` (for example, `feat(app): enable outcome-first runs`) and becomes the initial forge pull request title unchanged, without an id or slug prefix; a Lead may set a later title with `posse retitle` (section 14).
 2. Checks `max_workers` (`worker_limit`). A Worker slot is held by every `spawning`, `working`, `needs-decision`, `blocked` or `stalled` Task and by every Change Task in `done` or `merging`, because its Worker stays live and can return to `working` for CI fixes, merge-target syncs and Lead instructions. A slot is released only when the Task leaves those states (merged, torn down, failed or lost), so a follow-up never exceeds `max_workers` and queued refill never takes a slot a PR follow-up needs. The cost is that Change Tasks awaiting merge delay queued Tasks. Review Round Tasks skip this check and are never refused or queued for capacity, because the merges that release done Change Tasks depend on those rounds.
 3. Resolves the Profile.
-4. Chooses the next unused Task sequence, skipping existing Task history and `tasks/t<n>/` directories, and checks the Task-bound branch `posse/t<n>` against Task history, local refs and origin refs in the repository or selected workspace Members. A collision returns `branch_exists` before Task creation; a concurrent sequence change refuses admission and can be retried. Unrelated workspace Member failures or collisions do not block admission. It validates explicit `base:` and `target:` branches on origin without fetching, resolves config and selected-Member overrides, and freezes each target name and base commit on the Task's plan. Per repository, an explicit `base:` wins; without one, a selected target (Brief `target:`, `repositories.<member>.target_branch` or `defaults.target_branch`) other than the default branch is also the base, frozen at admission like an explicit base, except that it stays live behind a `landed` prerequisite. A base may be another published Task branch to form a stack. `posse start` prints `branches` with each repository's `base`, `base_commit` and `target`, and adds a warning for each base that differs from its target. The Task's `seq`, stored `branch` and `start` intent are committed together; except for explicit maintenance adoption, the stored branch must match its allocated `seq`. It acquires a Sandbox (section 12), checks out `posse/t<n>` with `git checkout -b` (never `-B`) from the pinned base commit, the live target-derived base, or the fresh default branch (Change Review: from the Change branch; Report Review: from the Project default branch). Repository Sandboxes open through `worktree.open` as linked child workspaces of the Lead; Workspace Project Sandboxes open as tabs in the Lead workspace. Posse reports pane `display_agent` using the resolved harness, then labels the pane with its Task identity ([ADR 0039](adr/0039-repository-workers-open-as-herdr-worktree-children.md)). The Lead's workspace comes from a fresh snapshot: the one